---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/copper`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Copper Reference',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamins & Minerals', item: `${SITE}/vitamins` },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Copper', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Copper: Ceruloplasmin, Zinc-Induced Deficiency, and Wilson's Disease"
  description="Copper reference — RDA, ceruloplasmin, the zinc-induced myelopathy trap, Menkes and Wilson's disease, and food sources."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamins", label: "Vitamins & Minerals" }, { href: "/copper", label: "Copper" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Copper</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Copper is a trace mineral required for iron transport (via ceruloplasmin),
      mitochondrial cytochrome c oxidase, neurotransmitter synthesis, and connective
      tissue crosslinking. Both deficiency and excess have distinct clinical syndromes;
      the main real-world deficiency scenario is <strong>zinc-induced copper deficiency</strong>,
      which is under-recognised.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Daily requirements</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li><strong>Adults:</strong> 900 µg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> 1,000 µg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Lactation:</strong> 1,300 µg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Tolerable Upper Intake Level:</strong> 10,000 µg/day.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Zinc-induced copper deficiency</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Chronic zinc intake above ~50 mg/day induces metallothionein in the intestinal
      mucosa, which preferentially binds copper and prevents its absorption. Case
      reports abound of adults developing severe copper deficiency causing myelopathy
      (progressive spastic gait), sensory ataxia, and cytopenias after months to years
      of high-dose zinc — sometimes from denture-fixation creams that contained zinc,
      more commonly from OTC zinc lozenges taken for immunity. If you take zinc &gt; 25
      mg/day chronically, take a copper supplement too (1–2 mg copper per 15 mg zinc).
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Copper deficiency signs</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>Microcytic or normocytic anaemia unresponsive to iron</li>
      <li>Neutropenia</li>
      <li>Myelopathy — spastic gait, sensory ataxia, paraesthesia</li>
      <li>Osteoporosis (from impaired lysyl oxidase, a copper-dependent enzyme)</li>
      <li>Hypopigmentation of hair and skin</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Wilson's disease</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Autosomal recessive mutation of the ATP7B copper transporter causes copper
      accumulation in the liver, brain, and cornea. Presents with liver failure, movement
      disorders, and Kayser-Fleischer corneal rings. Treated with copper-chelation
      (penicillamine, trientine) or zinc (which blocks copper absorption — the same
      mechanism that causes iatrogenic deficiency in healthy adults).
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Menkes disease</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      X-linked ATP7A mutation causing severe copper deficiency in infancy. Kinky-hair
      appearance, progressive neurologic decline, connective tissue defects; usually
      fatal in early childhood despite copper histidinate treatment. Very rare.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Food sources</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>Beef liver (3 oz): 12,400 µg — huge overshoot; a small serving covers a week's needs</li>
      <li>Oysters (3 oz cooked): 4,850 µg</li>
      <li>Dark chocolate 70–85% (1 oz): 500 µg</li>
      <li>Cashews (1 oz): 630 µg</li>
      <li>Sunflower seeds (¼ cup): 610 µg</li>
      <li>Lentils (1 cup cooked): 500 µg</li>
      <li>Shiitake mushrooms (½ cup): 650 µg</li>
      <li>Crab (3 oz): 670 µg</li>
    </ul>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> New numbness, unsteady gait, or unexplained
      anaemia in someone taking chronic high-dose zinc — check serum copper, ceruloplasmin,
      and stop the zinc. Progression to myelopathy can be irreversible.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/copper" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
